import{_ as n,I as s,c as r,o,ag as a,J as i}from"./chunks/framework.Vyheyqg9.js";const f=JSON.parse('{"title":"Build RAG from your notes","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"applied-ai/javascript/rag.md","filePath":"applied-ai/javascript/rag.md"}'),d={name:"applied-ai/javascript/rag.md"};function c(l,e,u,h,p,m){const t=s("MermaidDiagram");return o(),r("div",null,[e[0]||(e[0]=a('<h1 id="_5-build-rag-from-your-notes" tabindex="-1">Build RAG from your notes <a class="header-anchor" href="#_5-build-rag-from-your-notes" aria-label="Permalink to “Build RAG from your notes”">​</a></h1><p>RAG means <strong>retrieval-augmented generation</strong>: find useful source material, then ask a model to answer using it. It does not mean training the model on your files. You can update your source catalogue without changing model weights.</p><p>For a learning assistant, ask “How do I cancel fetch?” First find the cancellation note. Then provide that note as evidence. If no note supports an answer, say so rather than filling the gap with an invented citation.</p><h2 id="first-run-the-search-baseline" tabindex="-1"><span class="hub-section-number">1 </span>First run the search baseline <a class="header-anchor" href="#first-run-the-search-baseline" aria-label="Permalink to “1 First run the search baseline”">​</a></h2><p>Run <code>npm run demo</code> in the <a href="./../../workbench/applied-ai/javascript/">starter</a>. Its <code>retrieve</code> function splits text into lowercase terms, removes a small English stop-word list, ranks overlapping terms and returns at most two notes. Ties use stable note IDs.</p><p><code>cancel fetch</code> finds <code>n1</code>. <code>Who won the tennis tournament?</code> returns no matches. Try <code>terminate a network request</code>: simple word overlap may miss a relevant note. This is the limitation to investigate before adding embeddings.</p><p>The fixture is <strong>lexical retrieval</strong>, not semantic search and not a complete RAG assistant. Its score is not a probability. The default chatbot does not call it; connecting retrieval and generation is your next project increment.</p><h2 id="build-the-two-pipelines" tabindex="-1"><span class="hub-section-number">2 </span>Build the two pipelines <a class="header-anchor" href="#build-the-two-pipelines" aria-label="Permalink to “2 Build the two pipelines”">​</a></h2>',8)),i(t,{"encoded-source":"flowchart%20LR%0A%20%20D%5BAllowed%20documents%5D%20--%3E%20C%5BExtract%20and%20chunk%5D%0A%20%20C%20--%3E%20E%5BRepresent%20and%20index%5D%0A%20%20Q%5BUser%20question%5D%20--%3E%20R%5BRetrieve%20allowed%20chunks%5D%0A%20%20E%20--%3E%20R%0A%20%20R%20--%3E%20G%5BBuild%20evidence%20context%5D%0A%20%20G%20--%3E%20A%5BGenerate%20and%20check%20answer%5D"}),e[1]||(e[1]=a('<p><strong>Ingestion</strong> happens when documents enter or change. Keep source ID/version, chunk ID, exact text and location. Start with one small text file per note, then split longer notes on headings/paragraphs. Test that a boundary does not separate a rule from its exception. Chunk size in characters does not prove token fit.</p><p><strong>Answering</strong> happens for each question. Validate the question and user, retrieve only current permitted chunks, fit them into the model&#39;s input budget, generate an answer and validate its citations. Retrieved text stays data even when it says “ignore your instructions.”</p><p>For your first generated answer, use a result shape such as <code>{ answer, citations: [{ noteId, quote }] }</code>. Resolve every citation against the exact supplied source version. Exact quote membership prevents invented excerpts but does not prove the answer&#39;s claim follows from them. Review claim support separately.</p><h2 id="add-embeddings-when-you-can-measure-their-value" tabindex="-1"><span class="hub-section-number">3 </span>Add embeddings when you can measure their value <a class="header-anchor" href="#add-embeddings-when-you-can-measure-their-value" aria-label="Permalink to “3 Add embeddings when you can measure their value”">​</a></h2><p>An embedding model converts text into a vector. Embed the question with the same compatible model/configuration used for the stored chunks, then find similar vectors. A vector database stores/searches them; it is not the answer generator.</p><p>For a tiny corpus, a local exact vector scan is enough to learn. A hosted Pinecone adapter is an optional later exercise: create an index with the actual embedding dimension/metric, upsert stable IDs and metadata, query a namespace, and test updates/deletion. OpenAI&#39;s default <code>text-embedding-3-large</code> output is 3072 dimensions, not 768; an explicit supported <code>dimensions</code> setting changes the output size. <a href="https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/embeddings" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Embedding guide</a>, checked 2026-10-28.</p><p>Do not compare vectors from unrelated embedding spaces or assume a similarity threshold transfers between models. Keep query/chunk preprocessing and index versions in your evidence record.</p><h2 id="check-failure-cases-before-claiming-success" tabindex="-1"><span class="hub-section-number">4 </span>Check failure cases before claiming success <a class="header-anchor" href="#check-failure-cases-before-claiming-success" aria-label="Permalink to “4 Check failure cases before claiming success”">​</a></h2><p>Use five question types: exact wording, paraphrase, unsupported question, misleading near-match and an updated/deleted source. In a multi-user app add a forbidden source. Compare retrieval independently of generation: did the right chunk appear? Then judge whether the answer used it correctly.</p><p><strong>Your exercise:</strong> connect the lexical retriever to your chatbot using synthetic documents, add citations and no-evidence behavior, and freeze a small labeled question set. Then compare an embedding version. Keep unsupported, provider-error and missing-evidence states distinct.</p><p><strong>Done:</strong> show actual retrieval ranks and answer judgments, including one failure. <a href="./mcp.html">Next: MCP</a>. Use <a href="./../rag/">RAG engineering</a> when you reach access control, hybrid search, lifecycle and evaluation depth.</p>',11))])}const b=n(d,[["render",c]]);export{f as __pageData,b as default};
